  "title": "Store food as a packed lunch: fed foragers go back out",
  "question": "Does the right-hand colony read as ants working the route to the pile? Anything look wrong (ants heading the wrong way, bunching, stuck)?",
  "context_md": "Colony bed, food pile (red, right) 90 cells from the nest (left), seed 5, frames 8,000-14,000, one frame every 100. Ants are magenta while empty and cyan while carrying food.\n\n- **Left: today's ant.** Foragers make 1-3 trips, then stay at the nest holding store food; the drive to go out does not reach them because that food does not count as a packed lunch.\n- **Right: store lunch on.** Food taken near the nest (before an ant has been 8 cells from it) counts as a lunch, so a fed forager holding it is sent back out and eats it on the way.\n\nOver 24 seeds (no door): food taken from the pile 4,574 -> 19,732 (more on all 24), trips per ant per unit time 0.78 -> 1.46, share of a forager's life spent on trips 25% -> 43%, starved per ant that lived ~15% -> 5%. I am unsure about one thing: many ants now spend time west of the nest or past the pile rather than on the route.",
